## The path/filepath Package

Use `path/filepath` (not `path`) for file system paths because it automatically handles OS separator differences (`/` vs `\`).

## Basic Path Operations

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"path/filepath"
)

func main() {
	// Join paths (cross-platform)
	p := filepath.Join("home", "user", "documents", "file.txt")
	fmt.Println(p) // home/user/documents/file.txt (Linux/Mac)
	               // home\user\documents\file.txt (Windows)

	// Directory of path
	dir := filepath.Dir(p)
	fmt.Println(dir) // home/user/documents

	// File name only
	base := filepath.Base(p)
	fmt.Println(base) // file.txt

	// File extension
	ext := filepath.Ext(p)
	fmt.Println(ext) // .txt

	// Name without extension
	name := base[:len(base)-len(ext)]
	fmt.Println(name) // file

	// Absolute path
	abs, _ := filepath.Abs("relative/path")
	fmt.Println(abs)
}
```

## Creating Directories

```go
// Create a single directory
if err := os.Mkdir("mydir", 0755); err != nil {
	fmt.Println("Error:", err)
}

// Create a directory and all its parents
if err := os.MkdirAll("a/b/c/d", 0755); err != nil {
	fmt.Println("Error:", err)
}
```

## Reading Directory Contents

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"os"
)

func main() {
	entries, err := os.ReadDir(".")
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error:", err)
		return
	}

	for _, entry := range entries {
		if entry.IsDir() {
			fmt.Printf("[DIR]  %s\n", entry.Name())
		} else {
			info, _ := entry.Info()
			fmt.Printf("[FILE] %s (%d bytes)\n", entry.Name(), info.Size())
		}
	}
}
```

## filepath.WalkDir — Recursive Traversal

```go
err := filepath.WalkDir(".", func(path string, d os.DirEntry, err error) error {
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	if !d.IsDir() && filepath.Ext(path) == ".go" {
		fmt.Println("Go file:", path)
	}
	return nil
})
```

## Other File Operations

```go
os.Remove("file.txt")          // delete a file
os.RemoveAll("dir/")           // delete a directory recursively
os.Rename("old.txt", "new.txt") // rename/move a file
os.Stat("file.txt")            // file info (size, mode, etc.)
```

